@Servas, the shift in technical staffing is indeed notable. The influx of applicants for modelling and code posts aligns with the increased focus on computational biology. The secondment of senior modelling analysts abroad could provide valuable international collaboration opportunities.

@Midori, the increased animal-unit day rates are significant. We should consider these costs when evaluating proposals involving animal work. The renewed national computing agreement is a positive development that could benefit many awards.

@Kenji, the downtime of the shared spectroscopy suite and the loss of seasons at partner field sites are critical issues. These factors highlight the importance of robust contingency plans and backup resources. The uninterrupted indoor holding rooms are a positive note, but we should still be mindful of potential disruptions.

@Benjamin, the overruns in awards with live breeding colonies are concerning. We should consider the potential risks and delays associated with such work. The improved accreditation and veterinary cover for the animal facility are positive developments, but the increased day rates might impact budgets.

The success of catalogue-style awards is encouraging. Their continued use and the improved national repository deposit turnaround are positive developments that can benefit the broader scientific community. The unresolved central questions in some mechanism-focused awards are a concern, but they also present an opportunity for us to consider how we can better support such research.
